<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">All-Russian Central Executive Committee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The All-Russian Central Executive Committee was the standing body of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-congress-of-soviets">All-Russian Congress of Soviets</a>, exercising legislative power between its sessions from 1917 to 1937. Chaired briefly by Lev Kamenev, then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakov-sverdlov">Yakov Sverdlov</a> and, from 1919, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-kalinin">Mikhail Kalinin</a>, it was formally the highest organ of the Russian Soviet Republic, although real power lay with the Communist Party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/all-russian-central-executive-committee.jpg" alt="All-Russian Central Executive Committee" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeromi Mikhael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_Russian_Soviet_Federative_Socialist_Republic_(1920%E2%80%931954).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The All-Russian Central Executive Committee was first formed in 1917 as the permanent body of the First All-Russian Congress of Soviets of Workers' and Soldiers' Deputies, held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>. The congress elected a committee of 320 deputies: 123 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a>, 119 Social Revolutionaries, 58 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, 13 United Social Democrats and 7 others. Its chairman was the Menshevik Nikolay Chkheidze. This first committee was not a governing body, and Chkheidze was not head of the Russian state. After the July events it supported the Provisional Government, welcomed the appointment of the Social Revolutionary Aleksandr Kerensky as head of the government and decided to recognise its unlimited powers. It sat in the Tauride Palace until August 1917 and then moved to Smolny.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The committee tried to counteract the Bolshevization of the Soviets in the autumn of 1917. Its Social Revolutionary and Menshevik majority refused to recognise a congress of the Northern Region, held on 24–26 October in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>, and declared the planned Second Congress of Soviets illegal, yet agreed to convene it, postponing the opening from 20 October to 25 October (7 November in the new style). The congress opened on 7 November at 10:40 pm, in the midst of the armed uprising in Petrograd. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> and Social Revolutionaries condemned the Bolshevik actions as an illegal coup, and the old committee called the congress a private meeting of Bolshevik delegates.</p><p>On 8 November Lenin proposed dissolving the old committee and electing a new one. Of its 101 members, 62 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and 29 Left Social Revolutionaries; moderate socialists boycotted it. Lev Kamenev became chairman. On 28 November it merged with the executive committee of 108 elected at the Extraordinary Peasants' Congress, after which the Left Social Revolutionaries joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a> in a coalition with the Bolsheviks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The 1918 Constitution required the committee to convene the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-russian-congress-of-soviets">All-Russian Congress of Soviets</a> at least twice a year. A full congress elected it, with no more than 200 members, and it was wholly subordinate to the congress. It gave general direction to the government's policies, reviewed and adopted draft decrees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-peoples-commissars">Council of People's Commissars</a>, issued decrees of its own, and formed the Council and the People's Commissariats. State budgets were decided jointly with the congress.</p><p>Kamenev was chairman from 9 to 21 November 1917, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yakov-sverdlov">Yakov Sverdlov</a> from then until his death in office on 16 March 1919, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-kalinin">Mikhail Kalinin</a> from 30 March 1919. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was formed in December 1922, the chairmanship was mostly ceremonial. In 1937 the committee was replaced by the Presidium of the Supreme Soviet of the Russian SFSR.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “All-Russian Central Executive Committee” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Formally, supreme power belonged to the All-Russian Congress of Soviets and its <b>Central Executive Committee</b>, chaired by Yakov Sverdlov and, from 1919, Mikhail Kalinin, with the Sovnarkom as the government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
